#N canvas 20 20 7000 13260 12;
#X declare -path ../../Cut\ It;
#X text 20 20 tempo-bench -- the tempo acceptance run: the clock \, the transport \, the map and the aux LED. STEPPED BY HAND: press GO to run the step that has just been described \, press GO again to describe the next one. Nothing moves until you ask it to \, so the PASS IF is on screen and STILL while you read it. The prompt line always says what the next press will do., f 120;
#X text 20 150 GO ON THE MAC: the bng below \, or the dev panel's ENCODER CLICK -- u_mother-stub sends encbut unconditionally and nothing in Cut It consumes it. TURNING the encoder repeats the current step without advancing \, which is what to do when you looked away., f 120;
#X text 2400 200 ⚠️ GO ON THE DEVICE IS NOT THE ENCODER -- IT IS netcat \, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS NOT OPTIONAL. mother only forwards encbut once a patch has asked with /enableEncoder \, and NOTHING IN Cut It EVER ASKS -- m_organelle leaves the encoder out on purpose. So the click that drives this bench on the Mac is silently dead on the Organelle. Send instead with ./tools/go.sh from the Mac. ⚠️ NOT netcat: the one-liner this file used to print here does nothing on macOS -- BSD nc exits before the datagram is flushed at -w0 \, and -w1 was measured to fail too \, while the port IS bound and the bench is fine. It looks exactly like a dead bench. The device cannot send to itself either: busybox here has no nc at all. Asking mother to enable the encoder is NOT the fix either: that means writing to oscOut \, and g_oled is its sole owner., f 120;
#X text 20 280 Load it as a THIRD patch after mother.pd and main.pd. It touches nothing in the deployed patch -- it only pushes onto the same buses a controller would. A PASS IF is printed for EVERY step including the ones whose correct result is that nothing happens \, which are otherwise impossible to mark off., f 120;
#X text 2400 20 ON THE MAC EVERY ERROR STEP ALSO PRINTS /sdcard/cut-it-err.cur: write failed \, AND THAT IS NOT A FAILED STEP. u_err keeps its durable log on the Organelle's SD card \, which no Mac has -- so the write is attempted and refused every time. It is in fact the only proof off-device that the logging path ran at all. On the device that line must NOT appear \, and if it does the card is unwritable and the log is being lost silently., f 120;
#X obj 6000 40 r \$0-say;
#X obj 6000 100 print;
#X text 20 360 GO -- click here \, or the dev panel's encoder button. MAC ONLY -- on the device use netcat \, top right., f 60;
#X obj 20 420 bng 40 250 50 0 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -262144 -1 -1;
#X obj 20 500 s \$0-go;
#X obj 300 420 r encbut;
#X obj 300 480 select 1;
#X obj 300 540 t b;
#X obj 300 600 s \$0-go;
#X text 560 480 encbut sends 1 THEN 0 \, so select 1 is what stops one press counting twice., f 46;
#X obj 300 680 netreceive 9998 1;
#X obj 300 740 route go;
#X obj 300 800 t b;
#X obj 300 860 s \$0-go;
#X obj 1400 420 r enc;
#X obj 1400 480 t b;
#X obj 1400 540 s \$0-repeat;
#X text 1660 480 enc is 1 for up and 0 for down -- not plus or minus one -- so either direction repeats., f 46;
#X text 20 920 THE STATE MACHINE. phase 0 means the step has been described and not yet run \; phase 1 means it has run. SETTING THE STEP NUMBER IS WHAT DESCRIBES IT \, so advancing is just an increment -- there is no second path to keep in step., f 120;
#X obj 20 980 r \$0-go;
#X obj 20 1040 f;
#X obj 20 1100 select 0 1;
#X obj 300 1040 r \$0-set-phase;
#X obj 20 1170 t b b;
#X msg 300 1230 1;
#X obj 300 1290 s \$0-set-phase;
#X obj 20 1290 f;
#X obj 20 1350 s \$0-do-run;
#X obj 900 1170 t b b;
#X msg 1180 1230 0;
#X obj 1180 1290 s \$0-set-phase;
#X obj 900 1350 f;
#X obj 900 1410 + 1;
#X obj 900 1470 s \$0-set-step;
#X obj 1700 980 r \$0-set-step;
#X obj 1700 1040 t f f f;
#X obj 1700 1110 s \$0-set-inc;
#X obj 1980 1110 s \$0-set-run;
#X obj 1180 1350 r \$0-set-inc;
#X obj 300 1410 r \$0-set-run;
#X obj 2260 1110 s \$0-do-show;
#X obj 2100 980 r \$0-repeat;
#X obj 2100 1040 s \$0-do-show;
#X obj 2500 980 loadbang;
#X obj 2500 1040 del 500;
#X msg 2500 1100 1;
#X obj 2500 1160 s \$0-set-step;
#X text 20 1660 BEAT COUNTERS. \$0-zero resets one and starts a TEN SECOND window \; the count is latched and printed when that window closes \, and \$0-read reprints the latched value. THE WINDOW IS MACHINE-TIMED ON PURPOSE -- under manual stepping the gap between two steps is however long you take to judge one \, so a count taken between steps would mean nothing. On the Mac with DSP OFF every count reads 0 \, which looks exactly like a dead clock rather than a setting., f 120;
#X obj 20 1860 r clock;
#X obj 20 1930 t b;
#X obj 20 2000 f;
#X obj 180 2060 + 1;
#X obj 180 2120 t f f;
#X obj 420 2180 f;
#X obj 720 1860 r \$0-zero;
#X obj 720 1930 t b b;
#X msg 900 2000 0;
#X obj 720 2060 del 10000;
#X obj 420 2240 t f f;
#X obj 720 2300 f -1;
#X obj 20 2380 print M-BEATS;
#X obj 970 2240 r \$0-read;
#X text 20 2440 latch starts at -1 \, so a count read before its ten seconds are up says so rather than lying., f 46;
#X obj 1320 1860 c_clock 1 4;
#X obj 1320 1930 t b;
#X obj 1320 2000 f;
#X obj 1480 2060 + 1;
#X obj 1480 2120 t f f;
#X obj 1720 2180 f;
#X obj 2020 1860 r \$0-zero;
#X obj 2020 1930 t b b;
#X msg 2200 2000 0;
#X obj 2020 2060 del 10000;
#X obj 1720 2240 t f f;
#X obj 2020 2300 f -1;
#X obj 1320 2380 print C1-BEATS-ratio-1;
#X obj 2270 2240 r \$0-read;
#X text 1320 2440 latch starts at -1 \, so a count read before its ten seconds are up says so rather than lying., f 46;
#X obj 2620 1860 c_clock 1.5 4;
#X obj 2620 1930 t b;
#X obj 2620 2000 f;
#X obj 2780 2060 + 1;
#X obj 2780 2120 t f f;
#X obj 3020 2180 f;
#X obj 3320 1860 r \$0-zero;
#X obj 3320 1930 t b b;
#X msg 3500 2000 0;
#X obj 3320 2060 del 10000;
#X obj 3020 2240 t f f;
#X obj 3320 2300 f -1;
#X obj 2620 2380 print C2-BEATS-ratio-1.5;
#X obj 3570 2240 r \$0-read;
#X text 2620 2440 latch starts at -1 \, so a count read before its ten seconds are up says so rather than lying., f 46;
#X text 20 2580 DESCRIBING A STEP. One select per step rather than one wide fan \, so every cord stays short and local -- the same reason u_map chains its routes., f 120;
#X obj 20 2680 r \$0-do-show;
#X obj 20 2740 select 1;
#X obj 240 2740 t b b b;
#X msg 700 2740 === STEP-01-of-16 === baseline -- re-asserting 120 BPM and stopped;
#X msg 700 2790 PASS IF: two bars with a small gate mark under each and 120-bpm in the footer -- and the aux button lit DARK BLUE. This step exists so the run repeats without reopening the patch;
#X msg 700 2840 >>> press GO to run step 1 of 16;
#X obj 240 2910 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 3000 select 2;
#X obj 240 3000 t b b b;
#X msg 700 3000 === STEP-02-of-16 === COUNTING -- zeroing the beat counters for 10 s at 120 BPM;
#X msg 700 3050 PASS IF: NOTHING VISIBLE CHANGES. The transport is still stopped and the clock is running anyway -- which is exactly what is being counted;
#X msg 700 3100 >>> press GO to run step 2 of 16 -- then WAIT for the printed count before pressing GO again;
#X obj 240 3170 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 3260 select 3;
#X obj 240 3260 t b b b;
#X msg 700 3260 === STEP-03-of-16 === READ THE COUNTS -- the clock ran for 10 s while STOPPED;
#X msg 700 3310 PASS IF: M-BEATS 20 or 21 -- C1-BEATS the same number -- C2-BEATS 30 or 31. A zero anywhere means the clock does not run until the transport does -- or on the Mac it means DSP is off which looks exactly the same;
#X msg 700 3360 >>> press GO to run step 3 of 16;
#X obj 240 3430 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 3520 select 4;
#X obj 240 3520 t b b b;
#X msg 700 3520 === STEP-04-of-16 === START THE TRANSPORT;
#X msg 700 3570 PASS IF: the aux button turns GREEN and the 404 starts its pattern. WATCH EXT ON THE 404'S PATTERN SELECT SCREEN -- the number beside a pad is that SAMPLE's BPM and never moves;
#X msg 700 3620 >>> press GO to run step 4 of 16;
#X obj 240 3690 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 3780 select 5;
#X obj 240 3780 t b b b;
#X msg 700 3780 === STEP-05-of-16 === TEMPO THROUGH THE MAP -- og-knob-1 at 0 -- PICKUP HOLDS IT;
#X msg 700 3830 PASS IF: ⛔ THIS ONE DIFFERS BY MACHINE AND THE DIFFERENCE IS PICKUP WORKING. ON THE DEVICE the footer does NOT move -- mother restored knob 1 at boot and 0 is below that stored value so the knob is HELD until it crosses back. ON THE MAC nothing pushes knobs.txt at all so this is knob 1's first value ever -- it lands and the footer reads 10-bpm with the 404's EXT sliding down;
#X msg 700 3880 >>> press GO to run step 5 of 16;
#X obj 240 3950 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 4040 select 6;
#X obj 240 4040 t b b b;
#X msg 700 4040 === STEP-06-of-16 === TEMPO THROUGH THE MAP -- og-knob-1 at 1 -- THE CROSSING;
#X msg 700 4090 PASS IF: the footer reads 500-bpm and the 404's EXT slides up. ON THE DEVICE this is the moment the knob crosses the restored value and TAKES OVER -- held one step ago and live now. A slide rather than a snap IS the inference working -- it snaps only to a tempo it has already learned;
#X msg 700 4140 >>> press GO to run step 6 of 16;
#X obj 240 4210 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 4300 select 7;
#X obj 240 4300 t b b b;
#X msg 700 4300 === STEP-07-of-16 === TEMPO THROUGH THE MAP -- back to 0 -- LIVE ON BOTH MACHINES;
#X msg 700 4350 PASS IF: the footer reads 10-bpm on EITHER machine. Pickup has handed over so the knob tracks normally from here -- if this one is held too then the release never happened and the knob is stuck;
#X msg 700 4400 >>> press GO to run step 7 of 16;
#X obj 240 4470 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 4560 select 8;
#X obj 240 4560 t b b b;
#X msg 700 4560 === STEP-08-of-16 === OUT OF RANGE -- 5000 sent TWICE;
#X msg 700 4610 PASS IF: the footer reads 600-bpm and EXACTLY ONE alert appears -- a bordered box naming u_tempo. The second 5000 must be silent because the VALUE did not change;
#X msg 700 4660 >>> press GO to run step 8 of 16;
#X obj 240 4730 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 4820 select 9;
#X obj 240 4820 t b b b;
#X msg 700 4820 === STEP-09-of-16 === OUT OF RANGE THE OTHER WAY -- 0;
#X msg 700 4870 PASS IF: the footer reads 5-bpm and a SECOND alert appears. The verdict did not change but the value did -- and this is the case that was broken once;
#X msg 700 4920 >>> press GO to run step 9 of 16;
#X obj 240 4990 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 5080 select 10;
#X obj 240 5080 t b b b;
#X msg 700 5080 === STEP-10-of-16 === BACK TO 120;
#X msg 700 5130 PASS IF: the footer reads 120-bpm and NO alert appears at all;
#X msg 700 5180 >>> press GO to run step 10 of 16;
#X obj 240 5250 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 5340 select 11;
#X obj 240 5340 t b b b;
#X msg 700 5340 === STEP-11-of-16 === STOP -- and the clock must NOT stop with it;
#X msg 700 5390 PASS IF: the aux button goes DARK BLUE and the 404 stops -- but its display MUST STILL SAY EXT. If it falls back to BPM the clock stopped with the transport and that is the bug this step exists for;
#X msg 700 5440 >>> press GO to run step 11 of 16 -- then WAIT for the printed count before pressing GO again;
#X obj 240 5510 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 5600 select 12;
#X obj 240 5600 t b b b;
#X msg 700 5600 === STEP-12-of-16 === READ THE COUNTS WHILE STOPPED;
#X msg 700 5650 PASS IF: M-BEATS 20 or 21 again. Stop the pulse stream and the 404 stretches every sample to a stale tempo -- so this is the least obvious requirement in the phase;
#X msg 700 5700 >>> press GO to run step 12 of 16;
#X obj 240 5770 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 5860 select 13;
#X obj 240 5860 t b b b;
#X msg 700 5860 === STEP-13-of-16 === PANIC;
#X msg 700 5910 PASS IF: the aux button turns RED and the footer says panic -- and the clock is STILL running underneath;
#X msg 700 5960 >>> press GO to run step 13 of 16;
#X obj 240 6030 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 6120 select 14;
#X obj 240 6120 t b b b;
#X msg 700 6120 === STEP-14-of-16 === BY HAND -- press the aux button twice;
#X msg 700 6170 PASS IF: ON THE DEVICE use the real button. ON THE MAC use aux-tap and NOT the aux toggle -- aux is momentary 1 then 0 and only the 1 is a press -- so a toggle needs two clicks per press and the uncheck is meant to do nothing. First press GREEN and the 404 starts -- second DARK BLUE and it stops. If nothing happens at all then mother is eating the press;
#X msg 700 6220 >>> press GO to run step 14 of 16;
#X obj 240 6290 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 6380 select 15;
#X obj 240 6380 t b b b;
#X msg 700 6380 === STEP-15-of-16 === BY HAND -- sweep KNOB 1 all the way and back;
#X msg 700 6430 PASS IF: og-knob-1 appears as a parameter row on the OLED and the footer tracks between 10-bpm and 500-bpm. The 404 follows the sweep. ⚠️ A FULL SWEEP ALWAYS CROSSES so pickup can never make this step fail -- that is why it is a sweep and not a nudge;
#X msg 700 6480 >>> press GO to run step 15 of 16;
#X obj 240 6550 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 6640 select 16;
#X obj 240 6640 t b b b;
#X msg 700 6640 === STEP-16-of-16 === done -- press Ctrl-C;
#X msg 700 6690 PASS IF: every step above was marked off. If 2 3 11 or 12 failed the clock is wrong -- if 5 6 7 or 15 failed the map or pickup is -- if 4 13 or 14 failed the transport or the LED is;
#X msg 700 6740 >>> press GO to run step 16 of 16;
#X obj 240 6810 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 6900 t b;
#X msg 240 6900 === BENCH COMPLETE === every step has been run -- reload the patch to go round again;
#X obj 240 6960 s \$0-say;
#X text 20 7040 RUNNING A STEP. The actions fire in the order they are listed -- rightmost trigger outlet first -- and the line saying the step has fired goes out LAST \, off outlet 0 \, so it cannot arrive before the thing it describes., f 120;
#X obj 20 7140 r \$0-do-run;
#X obj 20 7200 select 1;
#X obj 240 7200 t b b b;
#X msg 480 7200 120;
#X obj 480 7245 s tempo;
#X msg 480 7310 bang;
#X obj 480 7355 s stop;
